{"study_accession":"PRJNA481499","secondary_study_accession":"SRP154136","title":"Gender-specific transcriptional profiles identified in β-thalassemia patients","description":"Lifelong treatment and major complications characterize β-thalassemia patients, whether they suffer from the milder type of the disease (β-thalassemia intermedia, TI) or the severe type (β-thalassemia major, TM). This study aimed to ascertain the differences in the transcriptional profiles of TI and TM patients to identify targets for stratification and the development of personalized therapeutic strategies. RNA-seq of 49 participants (16 TI, 16 TM, 17 healthy) identified a higher number of differentially expressed genes in TM than in TI patients when compared to healthy participants portraying accurately the clinical observations. When TI patients were compared to TM, the absence of significantly differentially expressed genes highlighted the increased variability seen in the TI expression profiles suggesting that a continuous spectrum describes the disease and not distinct conditions. Although no confounding effect was seen due to different centers, age groups and various clinical characteristics, when male and female patients were compared to healthy participants of the same gender, it was evident that males and females are affected in distinctive ways by β-thalassemia. TI triggers a pronounced and consistent transcriptional signature in males, whereas a small effect is seen in females reflecting increased biological variability. TM induces transcriptional changes of similar magnitude in both genders with limited overlap between them emphasizing that different pathways are affected. Our study provides a framework for further stratification studies in β-thalassemia and highlights the need to consider gender as an important variable for developing differential diagnostic and therapeutic strategies. Overall design: We have analysed the gene expression profiles of erythroid progenitor cultures derived from 16 TI and 16 TM patients using RNA-seq and we studied the differentially expressed genes and pathways irrespective to patient genotype. The aim of this study was to identify biomarkers for patient stratification and therapeutic management, identify confounding factors, pinpoint the targets that dictate each phenotype and possibly develop therapeutic strategies focused on these targets.","pubmed":"32817281","geo_series":"","n_samples":49,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN09669958","study_accession":"PRJNA481499","blood_class":"blood_cells","class_from":"sample","title":"Sample_18-TI_tripletT","description":"Sample_18-TI_tripletT","sex":"male","geo_sample":"GSM3288276","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2020-08-26","attributes":[["source_name","Thalassemia Intermedia_male_ErPCs"],["subject status","Thalassemia Intermedia"],["sex","male"],["tissue","peripheral blood"],["cell_type","Erythroid precursor cells (ErPCs)"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR7530272","experiment_accession":"SRX4398230","library_layout":"SINGLE","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina HiSeq 2000","read_count":32202305,"base_count":1610115250,"first_public":"2020-08-26","experiment_title":"Illumina HiSeq 2000 sequencing: GSM3288276: Sample_18-TI_tripletT Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":false},{"sample_accession":"SAMN09669959","study_accession":"PRJNA481499","blood_class":"blood_cells","class_from":"sample","title":"Sample_09-TM_tripletC","description":"Sample_09-TM_tripletC","sex":"female","geo_sample":"GSM3288247","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2020-08-26","attributes":[["source_name","Thalassemia Major_female_ErPCs"],["subject status","Thalassemia Major"],["sex","female"],["tissue","peripheral blood"],["cell_type","Erythroid precursor cells (ErPCs)"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR7530249","experiment_accession":"SRX4398207","library_layout":"SINGLE","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina Hi